{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790964609,"nonce":"1zESRsEqkr/zCx1U1/vqsGDorS8=","title":"Python: an embedder describes the exception it took from a module","message":"With take_pending_error (08b2b443) and parse_module_with_host (4995cd71) a host gets back the exception a Python call or module body raised, as the pending global's word. To raise it in its own language it needs the exception's type and text, which zb_any_type and zb_any_str give. Those are the module's own library code, and zb_any_type dispatches through the module's class hooks (zb_hook_instance_type), so a user exception class is only named correctly by the copy compiled with the module that declares it.\n\nA module parse_module_with_host lowers declares py$describe(x) -\u003e str (zyntax_python::DESCRIBE): the type's name, then \": \" and the text when there is any, as the uncaught report prints it. It is the module's own declaration, kept as an entry point, so the host takes its address once per module after compiling it. Caribou needs it to raise a Python exception in Haxe or Wren as an error naming it.\n\nSeen while testing: several TieredRuntimes in one process on parallel threads, each calling a function by its pointer, abort in osr::lazy_compile (\"could not be compiled\"), because the lazy compiler is process-wide. Calls by name do not. The tests that call by pointer run one runtime per process.","files":null}]}